health_u=[
([0,1],'A youth community centre offers free activities, but its last bus leaves before activities end. Option A advertises more widely; Option B changes finishing times to match transport.','Transport is a practical barrier despite free entry; the last bus leaves too early.','A timetable aligned with transport can enable participation; advertising alone does not remove the return-travel barrier.','Using accessibility, B addresses the barrier directly. A raises awareness but may attract people who still cannot attend. Changing times could inconvenience existing users.','B is defensible after consultation about times. Some users may still face cost or safety barriers, so transport alignment alone is insufficient.','Track attendance by transport access and ask non-attenders about barriers. Overall attendance could hide exclusion of particular groups.'),
([1,2],'A survey of 40 volunteers finds most like a new youth service. The community has 800 young people. Option A expands immediately; Option B consults non-users and underserved groups first.','The evidence may not represent the whole community; only 40 volunteers were surveyed.','Volunteers may have stronger interest or better access, biasing estimates of community needs.','Using representativeness, B improves evidence but delays expansion. A acts quickly using a narrow sample.','B is defensible to identify unmet needs before allocating resources. Consultation itself needs accessible methods and cannot assume one shared viewpoint.','Compare participation and feedback across users, non-users and relevant groups. Track who is missing from the sample rather than only its size.'),
([0,3],'A successful urban peer-support program is proposed for a rural community. Option A copies all delivery arrangements; Option B adapts location and access with local youth input. Rural travel distances are much longer.','Transfer needs to account for different local conditions, especially travel distance.','A delivery model can fail if the new context blocks access despite success elsewhere.','Using contextual fit, B can improve access but requires time and resources. A preserves the original format but may reproduce barriers.','B is defensible while preserving the program’s core purpose and evaluating adaptations. Local consultation may reveal needs not visible in the original evaluation.','Measure reach, engagement and relevant wellbeing outcomes in the new context, and document adaptations. Prior urban success alone is not evidence of rural effectiveness.'),
([1,2],'A council has trained youth workers and a free venue but limited awareness among new residents. Option A uses trusted community groups for outreach; Option B builds a second venue.','The main stated gap is awareness, while workers and a venue are existing community resources.','Trusted networks can connect residents with available resources without assuming physical capacity is the barrier.','Using efficient use of resources, A addresses awareness directly. B increases capacity at higher cost without necessarily increasing awareness.','A is defensible with accessible, culturally responsive communication. Trusted groups may not reach everyone, so use more than one channel.','Measure awareness and participation by subgroup, including new residents not linked to community groups. Count meaningful use rather than only publicity distributed.'),
([2,3],'A community activity pilot attracts 100 registrations but only 20 regular attendees. Option A reports registrations as success; Option B investigates attendance barriers before expanding.','Registration does not establish sustained participation; only 20 attend regularly.','Interest may not translate into use when timing, transport or belonging are barriers.','Using sustained access, B investigates actual engagement. A reports a positive output but risks overlooking the gap.','B is defensible with follow-up of attendees and non-attendees. Follow-up responses may still be biased towards reachable participants.','Track retention, reasons for non-attendance and participant-defined outcomes over time. Separate registrations, attendance and outcomes.')]
for i,(ut,*rest) in enumerate(health_u):decision_set('health',f'u-community-{i}',ut,None,*rest)
health_e=[
([0,1,3],'School leavers report uncertainty about setting boundaries in new relationships. Option A provides one information poster; Option B offers practical, inclusive communication workshops with referral information.','Young people need usable relationship skills during transition, beyond awareness alone.','Practising respectful communication and boundary-setting may support skills; information alone may not translate into action.','Using skill development, B provides practice and support at greater delivery cost. A is inexpensive and visible but passive.','B is defensible with safe facilitation and suitable referral pathways. Participation and self-reported confidence do not themselves prove safer relationships.','Assess reach, skill demonstration, acceptability and sustained use of support. Use ethical and confidential methods; reporting changes may reflect awareness.'),
([2,3,4],'A post-school support service launches a new app. Early users praise it, but many young people do not know it exists. Option A uses trusted peer ambassadors; Option B assumes word of mouth will be enough.','Adoption is limited by low awareness; early-user satisfaction is not the same as community reach.','Peer ambassadors can make an innovation visible and credible through social networks, encouraging trial.','Using diffusion and reach, A actively supports awareness but requires training. B costs less yet may spread mainly within already-connected groups.','A is defensible with diverse peer networks and accessible alternatives. Peer endorsement may not overcome poor app usability or digital exclusion.','Track awareness, trial, adoption and retention by subgroup. Downloads alone do not establish continued use or benefit.'),
([7,8],'A transition program reaches 300 of 1,000 eligible young adults. Participants report high satisfaction, but outcome data are absent. Option A declares effectiveness; Option B uses RE-AIM to evaluate further.','Reach is 30%, but effectiveness has not been demonstrated.','RE-AIM separates Reach, Effectiveness, Adoption, Implementation and Maintenance, preventing one positive measure from standing for all dimensions.','Using evaluation quality, B examines outcomes and delivery. A is quicker but confuses satisfaction with demonstrated health effect.','B is defensible with proportionate measures across all five dimensions. Evaluation cost and missing comparison data remain limitations.','Record eligible and participating populations, relevant outcomes, setting adoption, delivery fidelity and maintenance over time. Interpret changes in context.'),
([1,5,6],'A regional youth service is used mainly by university students. Apprentices report that opening hours clash with work. Option A increases campus promotion; Option B adds after-work access.','Opening hours restrict access for apprentices, creating uneven reach.','Aligning delivery with work patterns can remove an environmental barrier; extra campus promotion may widen the existing imbalance.','Using equity and uptake, B addresses a disadvantaged group but requires staffing. A may improve overall numbers without improving equitable access.','B is defensible after consultation about safe and workable access times. Hours alone may not remove transport or confidentiality barriers.','Compare reach and retention among apprentices and students and ask non-users about remaining barriers. Overall uptake can conceal inequity.'),
([3,6,8],'Young adults moving away from home identify isolation and unfamiliar services as concerns. Option A creates peer connections plus a service-navigation session; Option B sends a generic welcome email only.','The transition creates social and information needs; isolation and unfamiliarity are identified.','Peer networks may build social support while navigation information can improve access to resources.','Using relevance to needs, A addresses both concerns but takes coordination. B is low-cost but may be overlooked and offers little interaction.','A is defensible if voluntary, inclusive and responsive to local preferences. Not every participant wants the same kind of peer contact.','Track participation, perceived connection and successful service navigation over time. Self-selection and life changes limit causal conclusions.')]
for i,(et,*rest) in enumerate(health_e):decision_set('health',f'e-transition-{i}',None,et,*rest)
# History: self-contained source-method exercises, explicitly distinguished from topic knowledge.
history_cases=[
('A ruler’s public victory inscription, commissioned soon after a battle, states that the ruler defeated every opponent. No enemy account is supplied.','The inscription is useful evidence of the ruler’s desired public image and claim to legitimacy.','Its sponsor and celebratory purpose may encourage exaggeration or omission of losses.','Compare contemporary independent records, archaeological traces and accounts from other participants; seek agreement on specific claims rather than mere tone.','It supports a claim about official self-presentation, not a proven statement that every opponent was defeated.'),
('A private letter written during a crisis describes fear in one household. A later textbook claims the whole population was confident.','The letter provides a contemporary personal perspective on how the crisis was experienced.','One household cannot represent the whole population, and private writing can still be selective.','Compare letters from different groups, official records and the textbook’s evidence and date; investigate variation rather than assuming one account cancels the other.','The evidence challenges a universal claim of confidence by showing at least one fearful household, but does not establish the population-wide balance of feelings.'),
('Two newspapers give identical descriptions of an event. A note reveals both reproduced one government bulletin. An independent eyewitness differs on the number of participants.','The newspapers show how an official account circulated and how public knowledge may have been shaped.','They share one origin, so agreement is not two independent confirmations.','Trace provenance, compare the eyewitness’s opportunity to observe, and seek further independent records or material evidence about participation.','The repeated account supports a claim about dissemination, not independent confirmation of the participant count.'),
('Excavators find imported vessels in a settlement layer. A written source describes the settlement as completely isolated. The dating of some vessels is uncertain.','The vessels may provide evidence of exchange or contact if their origin and context are established.','Uncertain dating and possible later disturbance limit claims about contact in the exact period.','Check stratigraphy, scientific dating where appropriate, manufacturing origin and other trade evidence; examine what the written source meant by isolation.','The evidence raises doubt about complete isolation but cannot fix the timing or extent of contact without secure context.'),
('A memoir written 40 years after an event describes the author as central to its success. A contemporary attendance record does not list the author, but the record is incomplete.','The memoir reveals a later recollection and self-interpretation of participation.','Memory, hindsight and self-interest may affect it; absence from an incomplete record is not conclusive proof of absence.','Seek complete contemporary records, independent accounts and the author’s earlier statements; compare specific actions and chronology.','Centrality remains uncertain. A qualified conclusion recognises both the memoir’s claim and the incomplete record rather than asserting certainty.')]
for sid in ['ancient_history','modern_history']:
 for i,(stim,use,limit,corrob,conclusion) in enumerate(history_cases):
  prompts=[('use','Explain one way the supplied source could be useful for a historical inquiry.',use,[2],[3]),('limit','Explain one limitation of using this evidence to establish what happened.',limit,[2],[3]),('corroborate','Identify evidence needed to test the main claim. Explain how it would help.',corrob,[3],[4]),('argument','Write a qualified historical conclusion supported by the supplied evidence.',conclusion,[4],[6]),('inference','Distinguish an observation supplied in the scenario from a historical inference you could draw.', 'An observation is a stated source feature: '+stim.split('.')[0]+'. An inference goes beyond that feature: '+use+' The inference must remain qualified by provenance and context.',[1,2],[2,3])]
  for suffix,prompt,ans,ut,et in prompts:add(sid,f'source-{i}-{suffix}',prompt,ans,[('Accurate historical reasoning',1),('Specific use of supplied evidence',1),('Appropriate qualification or explanation',1)],ut=ut,et=et,stimulus='Hypothetical source-method exercise. Not an authentic historical source or evidence about your selected society.\n'+stim,note='Transferable source skills only. Use authentic sources and factual knowledge for your school-selected historical topic.')
# Evidence-guided topic essay questions: no invented answer for an unseen selected text/topic.
def essay_guides(sid,mode,indices=None):
 route=U[sid] if mode=='unit3' else E[sid]
 topics=route['topics'];indices=indices if indices is not None else range(len(topics))
 for i in indices:
  title=topics[i] if mode=='unit3' else topics[i]['title'];focus=[] if mode=='unit3' else topics[i]['focus']
  clean=title.replace('{option}','your selected text or topic')
  tasks=[
   (f'Develop an argument about {clean.lower()}. Support your argument with two precise examples from your studied material.', 'A strong response states a defensible answer to the question, uses two accurate and relevant examples, and explains how each supports the argument. '+('Address '+', '.join(focus)+'. ' if focus else '')+'A list of facts or a retelling does not establish an argument.'),
   (f'How does evidence complicate a simple interpretation of {clean.lower()}? Develop a qualified argument.', 'Identify a plausible simple interpretation, then use accurate counter-evidence or a contrasting perspective to refine it. Explain the significance of the tension; do not assume both interpretations are equally well supported.'),
   (f'Evaluate the significance of {clean.lower()} in its wider context. Use specific evidence.', 'Define what makes the feature significant, connect the evidence to the wider context and weigh its importance against another relevant factor. Conclude with a justified judgement rather than saying it is simply important.'),
   (f'Plan an extended response about {clean.lower()}. State a thesis and three linked analytical claims with evidence.', 'Provide a thesis that answers a focused question; sequence three distinct claims; attach an accurate example to each; explain the example’s relevance and how the claims build the thesis. '+('Use the focus areas '+', '.join(focus)+' where relevant. ' if focus else '')+'This is a planning response, not a full essay.')]
  for j,(prompt,ans) in enumerate(tasks):
   add(sid,f'{mode}-essay-topic-{i}-{j}',prompt,ans,[('Relevant and defensible central argument',1),('Accurate specific evidence selected from studied material',2),('Analysis or evaluation links evidence to the argument',2)],**({'ut':[i]} if mode=='unit3' else {'et':[i]}),level='hard',note='Text/topic-dependent essay practice. This is an answer guide, not a model answer for your selected material. Check facts and quotations against your class texts and sources.')
for sid in ['ancient_history','modern_history']:
 for mode in ['unit3','ea']:essay_guides(sid,mode)
# English and Literature retain their actual extended-response character.
for sid in ['english','english_add_lang','literature','english_lit_ext']:
 if sid in U:essay_guides(sid,'unit3')
 if sid in E:essay_guides(sid,'ea')
# Add supplied original close-reading extracts with genuinely checkable model interpretations.
literary=[
('When the committee asked who would speak, Mara folded her speech into a square small enough to disappear inside her fist. Outside, the crowd kept chanting her name.','Mara appears hesitant despite public expectation.','The folded speech and clenched fist make withdrawal physical; the chanting crowd intensifies the contrast between private reluctance and public demand.','Public confidence can coexist with private uncertainty.'),
('The old station clock had stopped at six. Every morning the commuters checked it anyway, then hurried towards trains that no longer came.','The place is represented as trapped in an outdated routine.','The stopped clock and habitual checking symbolise attachment to a former order; the final absence of trains undermines the routine.','Habit may persist after the purpose that sustained it has disappeared.'),
('We called the fence a welcome line. We painted flowers on the locked gate. From the other side, the children could see the paint but not the garden.','The collective speaker normalises exclusion through pleasant language.','The welcoming label and floral imagery contrast with the locked gate and restricted view, exposing the gap between official language and lived experience.','Attractive language can conceal unequal access.'),
('At first the river was a silver thread. By dusk it had unstitched the road, the oval and the careful map of where we belonged.','The river shifts from delicate scenery to a force that unsettles community certainty.','The extended sewing metaphor moves from thread to unstitched, showing water dismantling the human landscape and the assumed boundaries of belonging.','Human plans and identities can be destabilised by environmental forces.'),
('Dad said the empty chair gave us more room. He moved it closer to the table before every meal. None of us sat in it.','The father’s practical explanation masks continuing attachment to an absent person.','The repeated action of moving the chair contradicts his claim; the short final sentence gives absence a quiet collective weight.','Grief can be communicated through ordinary objects and routines rather than direct statements.')]
for sid in ['english','english_add_lang','literature','english_lit_ext']:
 for i,(extract,representation,tech,idea) in enumerate(literary):
  for j,(prompt,ans) in enumerate([
   ('Analyse the representation created in this extract. Use a specific detail.',representation+' '+tech),
   ('Explain how one stylistic choice shapes the extract’s meaning.',tech),
   ('Develop an interpretation of the extract’s central idea.',idea+' '+tech),
   ('Write a thesis that could guide an analytical response to this extract.', 'One defensible thesis is: '+idea+' The analysis should connect this claim to '+tech[0].lower()+tech[1:]),
   ('Explain how a contrast or tension develops meaning in the extract.',tech+' This supports the interpretation that '+idea[0].lower()+idea[1:])]):
   ut=[0,1,2] if sid in U else None
   et=[0,2,4,6] if sid!='english_lit_ext' else [0,3,4,5]
   add(sid,f'extract-{i}-{j}',prompt,ans,[('Defensible interpretation',1),('Specific textual evidence',1),('Explanation of how the evidence shapes meaning',2)],ut=ut,et=et,stimulus='Original practice extract, written for this resource; not from your prescribed text.\n'+extract,note='Close-reading skills practice on an original extract. This does not replace prescribed-text knowledge or a complete exam essay.')
# Essential English: five accessible media texts, five concrete reading questions each.
media=[
('SCHOOL REFILL DAY\nBring a refillable bottle this Friday. Free water at the library. One small swap can cut throwaway bottles.','students','bring a refillable bottle on Friday','One small swap','makes the action sound easy and manageable','Free water at the library','shows a practical way to take part'),
('LUNCHTIME WALKING GROUP\nNeed a break from your screen? Meet outside the hall at 12:30. Walk with a friend. Everyone is welcome.','students at school','join a lunchtime walk outside the hall at 12:30','Everyone is welcome','makes the group sound inclusive','Walk with a friend','suggests social support and company'),
('COMMUNITY BOOK SWAP\nYour finished story could be someone else’s new favourite. Bring one book. Take one book. Saturday, 9–11 am, community hall.','local readers','bring a book and swap it at the community hall on Saturday','someone else’s new favourite','presents a used book as valuable to another reader','Bring one book. Take one book.','gives two clear, balanced instructions'),
('CHECK BEFORE YOU SHARE\nAn amazing headline is not the whole story. Open the article. Check the date. Look for another reliable source. Share facts, not guesses.','people sharing information online','check an article before sharing it','Share facts, not guesses','uses a contrast to encourage careful sharing','Check the date','gives a concrete way to judge relevance'),
('LOCAL PARK CLEAN-UP\nHelp make our shared space shine. Gloves and bags provided. Sunday, 8 am, playground gate. Come for twenty minutes or stay for an hour.','local community members','help clean the park on Sunday morning','our shared space','builds a sense of shared responsibility','Come for twenty minutes or stay for an hour','offers flexibility and may make joining easier')]
for i,(text,audience,action,phrase,effect,detail,detail_effect) in enumerate(media):
 for j,(prompt,ans,pts) in enumerate([
 ('Who is this text for? Give one detail that helps you decide.',f'It is for {audience}. The location, action and wording fit that group; for example, “{detail}” is directed at people who could take part.',[('Audience',1),('Relevant detail',1)]),
 ('What does the text ask people to do?',f'It asks people to {action}.',[('Correct requested action',1),('Includes relevant event or checking detail',1)]),
 (f'What does “{phrase}” make the activity or idea sound like? Explain.',f'It {effect}. This encourages the intended audience to respond positively or take the requested action.',[('Explains the idea created',1),('Links the words to their effect',1)]),
 ('Choose one practical detail in the text. Explain how it helps the reader.',f'“{detail}” {detail_effect}. It helps the reader understand what to do or why taking part is manageable.',[('Specific detail',1),('Explains usefulness to reader',1)]),
 ('Explain the main message. Use one short example from the text.',f'The message is to {action}. “{phrase}” {effect}, supporting that message.',[('Main message',1),('Relevant short evidence',1),('Explains how it supports the message',1)])]):
  add('essential_english',f'media-{i}-{j}',prompt,ans,pts,ut=[0,1,2],stimulus='Original general media-reading practice. Not a released CIA topic or paper.\n'+text,level='easy',note='General CIA reading skills. Check your teacher-confirmed topic when it is released.')
philo_u=[
(0,'Define consequentialism.','Consequentialism assesses the moral status of actions by their consequences. Different versions specify different values or ways of evaluating outcomes.'),
(0,'Define deontological ethics.','Deontological approaches assess duties, rights or constraints on action, rather than treating the best aggregate outcome as the only test.'),
(0,'Explain the central concern of virtue ethics.','Virtue ethics emphasises character and practical judgement: what a virtuous person would perceive and do in context, rather than only a rule or outcome calculation.'),
(0,'Distinguish act and rule utilitarian reasoning.','Act utilitarianism evaluates the consequences of a particular act. Rule utilitarian reasoning considers rules whose general acceptance would promote utility; versions differ in detail.'),
(0,'Explain why pleasure and moral rightness are not synonyms in every ethical theory.','Some theories value wellbeing as an outcome, but others give independent weight to duty, rights or virtue. What feels pleasant is not therefore morally right under every framework.'),
(1,'Distinguish a premise from a conclusion.','A premise supplies a reason or evidence. A conclusion is the proposition that the argument asks us to accept on that basis.'),
(1,'Define validity in a deductive argument.','An argument is valid when it is impossible for all its premises to be true and its conclusion false. Validity concerns the inferential structure, not whether the premises are actually true.'),
(1,'Distinguish validity from soundness.','A sound deductive argument is valid and has true premises. A valid argument may have false premises and therefore be unsound.'),
(1,'Explain why an emotionally persuasive argument can still be weak.','Emotional force does not establish the truth of premises or the strength of the inference. Evidence and reasoning must still support the conclusion.'),
(1,'Identify the problem with rejecting a moral argument solely because its speaker is unpopular.','This attacks the person rather than assessing the argument’s reasons. The speaker’s unpopularity alone does not establish that the conclusion is false.'),
(2,'A policy benefits most people but seriously harms a small group. Identify one utilitarian question and one rights-based question.','A utilitarian asks about the overall balance and distribution of wellbeing consequences. A rights-based approach asks whether the harm violates a right or constraint even if aggregate benefit is positive.'),
(2,'A person keeps a difficult promise at a personal cost. Explain a duty-based justification.','The promise can generate a moral obligation. Keeping it can be justified by fidelity or respect for the other person rather than by immediate personal advantage.'),
(2,'Explain why a moral dilemma may involve conflicting duties.','An agent may have obligations that cannot all be fully satisfied in the circumstances, such as honesty and protection from serious harm. Ethical reasoning must address the conflict rather than merely naming one duty.'),
(2,'An action has a good intended outcome but foreseeable harm. Explain why intention alone may not settle its morality.','Consequences, duties and responsibility for foreseeable effects may matter as well as intention. A good intention does not by itself establish proportionality or permissibility.'),
(2,'Explain why applying an ethical theory requires more than naming it.','The response must state the relevant principle, connect it to the facts and show how it supports a judgement. Merely labelling a position utilitarian or deontological supplies no reasoning.'),
(3,'Distinguish a moral claim from a descriptive claim.','A descriptive claim says what is the case. A moral claim evaluates what ought to be done or what is right; descriptive facts alone require a normative bridge to justify it.'),
(3,'Explain the role of a counterexample in evaluating a moral principle.','A counterexample tests whether the principle produces an unacceptable or inconsistent judgement in a relevant case. It can justify revising the principle or limiting its scope.'),
(3,'Explain why disagreement does not by itself prove moral relativism.','People can disagree because of factual error, different reasoning or different values. The existence of disagreement does not logically establish that no objective moral truth is possible.'),
(3,'Distinguish consistency from truth.','A set of claims is consistent if it does not contain a contradiction. Consistency alone does not show that the claims accurately describe facts or establish a sound moral position.'),
(3,'Explain why a justified moral conclusion should address an important objection.','Addressing a serious objection tests the argument’s strength and clarifies its limits. Ignoring counter-reasons can leave a conclusion less well supported.'),
(0,'Explain impartiality in utilitarian reasoning.','Comparable interests are not given less weight merely because they belong to someone else. Impartiality still leaves questions about measuring wellbeing and comparing consequences.'),
(1,'All harmless acts are permissible. This act is harmless. State the conclusion and assess the argument’s form.','The conclusion is that this act is permissible. The form is valid: if both premises are true, the conclusion follows; soundness also requires defensible premises.'),
(1,'If an act is wrong, it causes harm. This act causes harm. Is concluding it is wrong deductively valid?','No. This affirms the consequent. The premise does not say that every harmful act is wrong; another condition might also produce harm.'),
(2,'Explain one challenge in predicting consequences before making an ethical decision.','Consequences can be uncertain, delayed and distributed across people. A reasoned judgement should use evidence, consider risk and avoid claiming perfect foresight.'),
(3,'Explain the difference between asserting a preference and justifying a moral judgement.','A preference states what someone likes. A justified moral judgement offers relevant reasons and principles that can be examined and challenged.')]
items('philosophy_reason',philo_u,'unit3',sources=['https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism/','https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-deontological/'])
philo_e=[
(0,'Explain philosophical anarchism’s challenge to political authority.','It questions whether states possess a justified general right to command obedience. Anarchist positions vary; they need not reject all voluntary cooperation or organisation.'),
(1,'Explain one central libertarian concern about redistribution.','Redistribution through coercive taxation can be criticised as infringing property rights and individual liberty. Versions of libertarianism differ, so the argument must be stated precisely.'),
(2,'Explain why social liberalism may support public education.','Education can expand people’s effective opportunities to exercise freedom. A social liberal may justify public provision to reduce structural barriers while retaining individual rights.'),
(3,'Explain one socialist criticism of concentrated private ownership.','Concentrated ownership can give a small group disproportionate economic and political power. Socialist approaches seek more collective or social control, though institutions and methods vary.'),
(4,'Distinguish negative and positive freedom.','Negative freedom concerns absence of interference. Positive freedom concerns self-direction or effective capacity to act; definitions and implications are contested.'),
(5,'Distinguish equality from equity.','Equality concerns sameness in a specified respect, such as legal status or resources. Equity concerns fairness in light of relevant differences; an argument must identify what is being equalised and why.'),
(5,'Explain why equal formal rights may coexist with unequal opportunities.','People may share the same legal permission yet face different resources, barriers or social conditions. Formal equality does not automatically equalise practical access.'),
(6,'Explain the role of an assumption in a political argument.','An assumption is a claim the reasoning relies on, sometimes implicitly. For example, a policy argument may assume people respond to incentives or have equal starting resources.'),
(7,'Explain why evaluating a political philosophy requires criteria.','Criteria such as liberty, fairness or justified authority make the basis of judgement explicit. An evaluation weighs how the philosophy meets them rather than merely describing its features.'),
(8,'Explain a possible conflict between individual and collective needs.','An individual may prefer unrestricted resource use while shared environmental protection requires limits. A political argument must justify whose interests are constrained and why.'),
(0,'Distinguish rejecting state authority from rejecting every rule.','A person can challenge coercive state authority while accepting voluntary agreements or community norms. The grounds and enforcement of rules remain important questions.'),
(1,'Explain why a libertarian may favour voluntary exchange.','Voluntary exchange can be seen as respecting self-ownership, property and freedom of choice. The argument depends on whether consent and background holdings are legitimate.'),
(2,'Explain a social liberal justification for limiting market outcomes.','Where market conditions undermine fair opportunity or essential capabilities, regulation or public services may be justified to support effective freedom and protect rights.'),
(3,'Distinguish a criticism of inequality from a complete socialist institutional proposal.','Identifying inequality does not specify who should own resources, make decisions or distribute outcomes. A full proposal must explain institutions and justify their operation.'),
(4,'A person is legally free to attend university but cannot afford it. Explain the freedom distinction involved.','Absence of a legal prohibition indicates negative freedom in that respect. Lack of resources may restrict effective opportunity or capacity, a concern often connected to positive freedom.'),
(5,'Explain why equal distribution may not be fair in every case.','Different needs or relevant circumstances can justify different allocations under some theories. A fair-distribution argument must state and defend its distributive principle.'),
(6,'Identify the problem with “This policy is popular, so it must be just.”','Popularity is not sufficient evidence of justice. The argument needs a normative premise connecting majority support to fair treatment and must address minority rights.'),
(7,'Explain one way to compare two political philosophies without misrepresenting them.','State each position in its strongest defensible form, apply common criteria and test both against the same case. Acknowledge internal variation and significant objections.'),
(8,'Explain why public benefit alone does not settle every restriction on liberty.','A restriction also requires examination of necessity, proportionality, fairness and authority under the relevant political theory. An asserted benefit does not automatically override all individual claims.'),
(0,'Explain one challenge for a society organised through voluntary cooperation.','It must explain how disputes, shared resources and non-cooperation would be managed without assuming an unquestioned state enforcement authority.'),
(1,'Explain one objection a social liberal might raise to a strict non-interference account of freedom.','Formal non-interference can leave serious resource and power barriers intact. The objection is that people may need enabling conditions to exercise meaningful choices.'),
(2,'Explain why supporting public services does not by itself imply rejecting individual liberty.','Public services may be justified as enabling freedom and opportunity. The argument must still consider coercion, costs and limits rather than assume all state action is liberty-enhancing.'),
(3,'Explain why “socialism means everyone receives exactly the same income” is an oversimplification.','Socialist traditions differ on distribution and institutions. Their central concerns about ownership and economic power do not require one identical-income rule in every version.'),
(7,'Explain how a counterexample can challenge a political principle.','A relevant case may reveal an implication inconsistent with the principle’s stated goals or with another commitment. The response should assess whether revision or a qualification is needed.'),
(8,'Explain what makes a recommendation about collective action justified.','It identifies the affected interests, applies explicit criteria, uses evidence, compares alternatives and answers a serious objection or trade-off.')]
items('philosophy_reason',philo_e,'ea',sources=['https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/libertarianism/','https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/liberty-positive-negative/'])
# Community self-determination cases are fictional and do not speak for any actual community.
atsi_cases=[
('An agency drafts a cultural program before consulting the relevant community. Option A holds one information meeting after decisions; Option B gives community-selected representatives authority from planning onward.','Self-determination is limited when decisions are already final before participation.','Real decision-making power can change priorities and accountability; receiving information alone is not shared control.','Using self-determination, B provides influence. A is quicker but consultation after decisions cannot shape them.','B is defensible with community-defined representation and paid participation. No single representative should be assumed to speak for everyone.','Ask the community how influence will be demonstrated and evaluate whether its priorities shape decisions. Attendance alone does not prove control.'),
('A public organisation proposes reconciliation action. Option A stages a single ceremony; Option B combines recognition with accountable employment and procurement commitments developed with relevant Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander stakeholders.','Symbolic recognition needs to be considered alongside sustained practical action.','Accountable commitments can connect recognition with institutional change, while a ceremony alone may leave barriers untouched.','Using sustained impact, B includes measurable commitments but requires resources. A is visible and immediate but limited in duration.','B is defensible if commitments are co-developed and resourced. Employment counts alone do not establish culturally safe conditions or shared authority.','Track agreed commitments, retention, workplace experience and stakeholder-defined outcomes. Evaluate progress with the relevant communities, not merely publicity.'),
('A museum plans an exhibition about a local community. Option A relies only on old administrative records; Option B seeks authorised community voices and relevant records, respecting permissions.','Representation risks being one-sided when only institutional records are used.','Different sources can reveal perspectives omitted from administrative records. Community authority and cultural permissions affect appropriate use.','Using representational fairness, B broadens evidence but requires time and relationships. A is easier but may reproduce institutional biases.','B is defensible with locally appropriate protocols. Community knowledge is not automatically available for public display and permissions must be respected.','Evaluate source diversity, permissions, community feedback and whose interpretations shape the exhibit. Visitor numbers alone cannot establish respectful representation.'),
('A service evaluation reports 500 contacts but no data about whether access barriers changed. Option A declares success; Option B agrees outcomes and evaluation methods with the relevant community.','Service contacts are outputs and do not establish improvements in access or outcomes.','Community-defined outcomes can direct evaluation towards changes that matter rather than institutional activity counts.','Using meaningful accountability, B tests outcomes. A is simpler but may confuse activity with impact.','B is defensible with respectful data governance and agreed indicators. Small samples and changing circumstances limit causal claims.','Track agreed access outcomes and qualitative experience, with community authority over data use. Compare with baseline while recognising context.'),
('A youth advocacy group asks for a role in a local policy review. Option A invites it to observe only; Option B enables submissions, accessible meetings and a published response to recommendations.','Participation requires opportunities to influence decisions rather than merely watch them.','Accessible processes and reasoned responses can make advocacy visible and decision-makers accountable.','Using participation, B provides an avenue for influence at additional administrative cost. A offers visibility but little agency.','B is defensible while respecting diversity of viewpoints and the group’s chosen priorities. An invitation alone does not guarantee recommendations are acted on.','Examine whether recommendations receive reasons and shape decisions, and ask participants about accessibility. Count influence as well as attendance.')]
for i,args in enumerate(atsi_cases):decision_set('atsi_studies',f'community-{i}',[2,3],[7],*args)
religion_cases=[
('A fictional faith-based organisation states that every person has dignity. It offers meals without requiring recipients to share its beliefs. Option A continues open access; Option B limits meals to members.','The organisation must consider consistency between its stated dignity principle and access.','Open access translates its stated universal dignity value into practical service beyond membership.','Using consistency with the stated belief, A aligns with every person’s dignity. B may reserve scarce resources but narrows access.','A is defensible from the supplied principle, with fair capacity management. This fictional statement cannot represent all adherents of any religion.','Track access, unmet need and recipient experience, not only meals served. Outcomes must be interpreted against capacity and local needs.'),
('A fictional religious environmental group values care for creation. Option A runs one awareness event; Option B combines teaching with monitored waste reduction at its venue.','The group needs to connect its stated ecological value to practical conduct.','Teaching can shape understanding, while a monitored reduction program can change institutional practices.','Using practical implementation, B tests action but needs resources. A may raise awareness without evidence of changed behaviour.','B is defensible if participation and measurement are feasible. The value’s meaning must be interpreted in the group’s context rather than attributed to all religions.','Measure waste before and after and document changes in practice. An event attendance count is not an environmental outcome.'),
('A fictional religious aid group says compassion guides its work. Its service opens only while many potential users are at work. Option A keeps the schedule; Option B consults users about access times.','Opening hours may conflict with the group’s compassion-based service aim.','Consultation can reveal practical barriers and inform a more accessible expression of the stated value.','Using access, B can improve fit but requires staff flexibility. A preserves routine yet may exclude intended users.','B is defensible subject to staffing and user needs. Claimed compassion alone does not establish actual effectiveness.','Track who can attend and who remains excluded, plus relevant outcomes. User feedback may not include people unable to access the service.'),
('Two fictional religious organisations share a concern for human wellbeing. One prioritises emergency relief; the other supports longer-term advocacy. A donor can fund only one this month.','The alternatives express a shared stated value through different time horizons.','Relief can address immediate needs; advocacy can target institutional causes, but its effects may be slower and uncertain.','Using urgency and sustainability, relief responds now while advocacy may seek systemic change. Neither is automatically superior in every context.','A recommendation should state the chosen priority and use evidence of need and feasibility. The brief supplies no comparative outcome data, so confidence must be qualified.','Compare unmet urgent needs, sustained outcomes and each organisation’s actual contribution. Activity counts cannot establish relative social influence alone.'),
('A fictional faith community debates a rights issue. Its leaders cite a shared teaching but reach different applications. Option A reports one view as universal; Option B explains the interpretive differences.','A shared tradition can contain diverse ethical interpretations.','Different contextual reasoning and interpretive priorities can produce different applications of a shared teaching.','Using accuracy, B represents diversity. A is simpler but erases disagreement and may misrepresent the tradition.','B is defensible with authentic sources and clear attribution. Internal variation must be evidenced rather than assumed to make every view equally influential.','Check whose views are represented, the sources used and their authority and context. One leader’s statement cannot establish every adherent’s position.')]
for i,args in enumerate(religion_cases):decision_set('study_religion',f'belief-action-{i}',[0,1,2,3,4],[7,8,9],*args)
# Arts: notation and descriptions are supplied; exercises are not substitutes for unseen audio/video.
def interpret_set(sid,i,stim,meaning,choice,alternative,development,limit):
 rows=[('identify','Identify two relevant choices in the supplied stimulus and explain how they work together.',choice+' '+meaning),
 ('analyse','Analyse how the supplied choices create meaning. Use specific evidence.',meaning+' '+choice),
 ('compare','Explain how the alternative below would change the effect. Compare it with the original.',alternative),
 ('develop','Propose a development or refinement and justify it using the supplied material.',development),
 ('evaluate','Evaluate how effectively the supplied choices support the stated interpretation. Identify a limitation.',meaning+' '+limit)]
 for suffix,prompt,ans in rows:
  add(sid,f'arts-{i}-{suffix}',prompt,ans,[('Specific and accurate use of the supplied choices',1),('Explains a relationship between choice and meaning',2),('Justified interpretation or judgement',1)],et=[i],stimulus='Original description/notation for practice; not an authentic performance or artwork.\n'+stim,note='Focused analytical practice. Descriptions do not replace examination audio, video, images or scores. Model interpretations are defensible examples.')
music=[
('A four-note motif C–D–E–G is repeated, then begins on G as G–A–B–D. The second statement is higher but preserves the interval pattern.','Repetition makes the motif recognisable while the higher sequence can build direction and intensity.','The rising contour and transposed motif link continuity with development.','If the second statement descended stepwise instead, continuity would rely less on exact interval repetition and the direction could feel less driving.','Extend the motif with a contrasting descending response, then return to the original to make the relationship audible.','Pitch movement alone does not fix an emotion; harmony, rhythm and performance context also matter.'),
('A steady 4/4 pulse underpins a melody with repeated off-beat accents. A later section places accents on each beat.','Off-beat accents create rhythmic tension against the regular metre; beat-aligned accents make the later section feel more settled.','The contrast depends on accent placement against an unchanged metric pulse.','If the accompaniment also abandoned the steady pulse, the contrast might become less clear because the reference pattern is weakened.','Preserve the pulse while varying accent density, then align accents for a clear resolution of rhythmic tension.','A written description cannot establish exact tempo, articulation or perceived groove.'),
('In C major, a phrase moves through F–G–C. A contrasting phrase ends on G without reaching C.','The dominant-to-tonic close supports tonal arrival, while ending on the dominant can leave expectation unresolved.','Harmonic function and phrase ending work together to create different degrees of closure.','Ending both phrases on C would reduce the difference in tonal closure; an unexpected chord could redirect the expectation.','Delay the final tonic with a held dominant before resolving, making the relationship between expectation and arrival clearer.','Interpretation depends on voicing, style and melodic context; chord labels alone are incomplete evidence.'),
('A solo voice begins unaccompanied. Two additional voices enter with the same melody at different times, creating overlapping lines.','The movement from one line to overlapping entries creates increasing textural complexity while retaining thematic connection.','Imitative entries and expanding vocal texture produce both unity and density.','Entering all voices simultaneously in the same rhythm would favour homorhythmic clarity rather than overlapping imitation.','Space successive entries so the motif remains audible, then thin the texture to restore focus.','Without audio or a complete score, balance and clarity can only be proposed rather than established.'),
('An ABA plan opens with a short lyrical section, introduces a rhythmically dense contrasting B section and returns to A with added ornament.','The return gives structural recognition while ornament makes it a changed return rather than a literal repetition.','Contrast, recurrence and variation organise expectation across the larger form.','Removing the final A would leave a contrasting binary trajectory rather than a return-based structure.','Let one rhythmic detail from B appear briefly in the returning A to connect contrast with synthesis.','Section labels alone do not prove successful expression; duration and actual musical detail must support the interpretation.'),
('A short piece combines a rising motif, increasing off-beat accents, denser texture and a delayed tonic arrival. Its stated intention is growing anticipation followed by release.','Several elements support anticipation: upward movement, rhythmic tension and increasing density; delayed tonal closure supports later release.','Pitch, rhythm, texture and harmony work cumulatively rather than as isolated labels.','Keeping the texture and harmony static would reduce two sources of intensification, even if the motif still rose.','Coordinate the strongest accent and highest density before the tonic arrival, then thin the texture on resolution.','A justified evaluation must test how the elements sound together; the stated intention does not guarantee the listener’s response.')]
for sid in ['music','music_ext_26_comp_syll.pdf','music_ext_26_music_syll.pdf','music_ext_26_perf_syll.pdf']:
 for i,args in enumerate(music):interpret_set(sid,i,*args)
arts={
'dance':[
('Three dancers travel on straight pathways at a sustained tempo, then contract into a low, tightly grouped shape.','The move from open travel to a compressed group can suggest restriction or increasing dependence.','Pathway, level, grouping and sustained timing establish the contrast.','If dancers expanded apart instead, the spatial trajectory could suggest release or fragmentation.','Repeat one straight pathway in the contracted group to preserve a movement link.','Meaning depends on movement detail and context, not one spatial shape alone.'),
('A reaching gesture returns three times: once as a solo, then in canon, then in unison.','Repetition creates a motif while canon and unison change the relationship between individual and group.','A recurring gesture is developed through temporal and group organisation.','Performing every return as the same solo would reduce the changing group relationship.','Vary the reach’s level on the final return to mark development while preserving recognition.','A description cannot establish precise timing or how clearly the motif is performed.'),
('Cold side lighting isolates a dancer while a low sustained sound continues. A warm full-stage wash begins when the group enters.','Production contrasts can reinforce movement from isolation towards collective presence.','Lighting extent, colour and sound duration frame the changing stage relationship.','A full warm wash from the beginning would weaken the visual isolation.','Time the lighting expansion to the group’s first shared gesture to connect production with movement.','Colour does not have one universal meaning; interpret it with choreography and context.'),
('A repeated balance becomes less stable across three phrases, followed by a grounded, controlled ending.','Changes in control and stability can communicate strain and later composure.','Balance, weight control and expressive phrasing make the progression visible.','Identical stability in every phrase would reduce evidence of change.','Specify the intended off-balance moments and recovery so the effect reads as controlled performance rather than error.','Expressive interpretation must consider technical intention and actual execution.'),
('The work begins with disconnected solos, develops through mirroring and ends with one shared phrase. Its stated theme is belonging.','The shift from separation to shared movement supports a representation of growing connection.','Group relationships, motif development and structure connect the theme across the work.','A final return to separate solos might complicate belonging by suggesting temporary or fragile connection.','Retain a recognisable individual gesture in the shared phrase to show belonging without complete sameness.','A theme label is not evidence; a full evaluation needs the actual movement and its effects.')],
'drama':[
('One actor stands on a raised platform and interrupts another actor seated below. Silence follows each interruption.','Status, space and tension represent an unequal relationship.','Levels, interruption and silence reinforce power and constrained response.','Placing both at the same level and allowing equal speech could weaken the hierarchy.','Let the seated actor stand during the final silence to make a shift in status visible.','Levels alone do not determine status; voice, action and context also matter.'),
('A scene repeatedly stops as actors step out of role to address the audience about conflicting accounts.','Direct address and interruption of the scene can invite critical distance and scrutiny of competing perspectives.','The convention changes the audience’s relationship with representation rather than presenting uninterrupted illusion.','Continuous naturalistic action would provide a different relationship, potentially reducing explicit reflection on the accounts.','Give each direct address a distinct evidential claim, then return to a changed action.','A named convention alone is not analysis; actual delivery and context must support the effect.'),
('An actor delivers a confident speech while avoiding eye contact and repeatedly twisting a sleeve.','Conflict between verbal confidence and physical unease can reveal insecurity or concealment.','Voice, gaze and gesture create subtext through contradiction.','Relaxed posture and sustained eye contact would make the confidence less visibly conflicted.','Build a controlled increase in the sleeve gesture at moments of pressure.','Avoid assuming gesture proves a single emotion; interpret it within the dramatic situation.'),
('A domestic table is placed inside a stark cage-like set. A narrow light excludes the audience from seeing the exit.','Familiar domestic imagery is constrained by the surrounding design, supporting an interpretation of entrapment.','Set symbolism and restricted lighting reframe the apparently ordinary place.','An open bright exit would suggest greater possibility of escape and change the tension.','Reveal the exit briefly at a turning point, connecting design to a meaningful shift in action.','The interpretation needs actual production evidence; a symbolic object is not automatically effective.'),
('A scene begins with shared dialogue, breaks into isolated monologues and ends with the actors facing away from one another. The stated focus is fractured trust.','The structural and spatial movement from exchange to separation supports fractured trust.','Dialogue form, actor-audience relationship and final image develop the focus.','Returning to shared dialogue at the end could suggest repair or an uneasy reconciliation.','Echo one earlier shared phrase in the final monologues to show what has been lost.','A strong evaluation weighs cohesion and clarity across the actual performance, rather than accepting the focus statement as proof.')],
'film_tv':[
('A character is first filmed in a wide shot at the edge of a crowded frame, then in a tight close-up after speaking.','Framing moves attention from social marginality to individual experience or agency.','Scale and placement change the spectator’s access to the character.','A central close-up throughout would remove the visible change from marginal position.','Hold the wide shot before cutting so the shift in attention is perceptible.','A close-up does not automatically create sympathy; performance and context matter.'),
('A sequence alternates a person waiting with a clock and an approaching bus. Shot duration becomes shorter.','Cross-cutting and accelerating rhythm can build anticipation about whether the arrival will occur in time.','Temporal juxtaposition and shortening duration connect separate spaces into one developing event.','Long unbroken shots could slow urgency and emphasise duration rather than convergence.','Reserve the fastest cuts for the point of greatest uncertainty, then extend the arrival shot.','The actual pace depends on shot lengths and action, not a statement that editing is fast.'),
('A cheerful song plays over images of an abandoned playground. The song stops abruptly on a broken swing.','Contradiction between sound and image can unsettle an apparently cheerful surface; abrupt silence can direct attention to absence.','Counterpoint and a sound transition alter interpretation of the same location.','Matching mournful music would make the emotional cue more direct and less contradictory.','Let a playground sound briefly emerge after the music stops to connect present absence with implied memory.','Interpretation depends on the song’s associations and the wider sequence.'),
('A spotless office contains one wilted plant. The character straightens every object except the plant.','Set detail and behaviour can complicate an image of control by leaving a sign of neglect or vulnerability.','Props, arrangement and repeated action create a visual contrast.','Removing the plant would reduce that contrast and make the office more uniformly controlled.','Return to the plant after a narrative turning point to develop its significance.','A prop’s meaning must be established through context rather than assumed from its presence.'),
('An online diary begins with polished frontal shots but later includes handheld, interrupted recordings.','Changes in recording style can represent a shift from curated self-presentation towards instability or immediacy.','Narrative progression and stylistic contrast construct identity over time.','Keeping the polished style throughout would preserve control and change the identity trajectory.','Repeat a phrase across the polished and handheld recordings to expose changed meaning.','Handheld footage is still constructed and is not automatically truthful.'),
('A character’s account uses warm close-ups, while a contrasting account uses distant static images. Both describe the same event.','Contrasting technical choices can position audiences differently towards competing versions of an event.','Framing, colour and camera movement shape viewpoint rather than merely documenting facts.','Giving both accounts identical visual treatment could reduce stylistic bias while leaving verbal differences.','Introduce an image that complicates each account so evaluation is not determined by style alone.','Audience positioning is an interpretation supported by choices, not proof of every viewer’s response.')],
'visual_art':[
('A rectangular composition places a small dark figure against a large pale field. A diagonal red line leads towards the figure.','Scale contrast and directional line can make the figure seem isolated yet visually significant.','Relative scale, value contrast and diagonal movement organise attention.','Enlarging the figure to fill the frame could reduce the sense of isolation.','Test line placement so it directs attention without overwhelming the figure.','Colour associations vary; support an interpretation through relationships within the composition.'),
('An artist builds a surface from torn paper, transparent paint and exposed stitching.','Layering, translucency and visible joins can suggest accumulated experience or repair.','Material properties and process traces contribute to meaning.','A smooth opaque surface would hide layering and the evidence of joining.','Preserve selected seams as focal points while varying transparency to reveal earlier layers.','Material choices need contextual evidence; stitching does not carry one fixed meaning.'),
('An artist’s statement says the work explores belonging; repeated door shapes are partly open and partly blocked.','The contrasting thresholds support an interpretation of belonging as conditional or negotiated.','Repetition and variation connect the stated intention to visual evidence.','Making every doorway fully open would simplify the tension towards access.','Develop differences in scale or material across thresholds to explore unequal access.','The artist’s intention informs interpretation but does not replace analysis of the work.'),
('A contemporary image reworks a familiar formal portrait by placing repair tools in the sitter’s hands.','The reworking can question whose labour and status deserve representation.','Portrait conventions and unexpected objects create meaning through contextual contrast.','Removing the tools would weaken the visible challenge to the conventional status image.','Research the original portrait conventions and document the new objects’ contextual significance.','Contextual interpretation requires reliable information about the source conventions and the work.'),
('Work A shows a pristine industrial skyline; Work B uses rusted fragments to construct a broken skyline.','The works offer contrasting representations of industry through image and material.','Idealised surface versus damaged material can support different attitudes to progress or its costs.','Using polished materials in B could complicate or weaken the contrast with A.','Compare composition, process and context before judging how each communicates its view.','An evaluation needs criteria and evidence rather than assuming rough material is inherently more critical.')],
'design':[
('A brief requires a reusable lunch container that is repairable, washable and under $25. Concept A is glued shut; B has replaceable clips.','Repairability is better supported by replaceable components, while cost and washing performance require testing.','The requirement and joining method connect design detail with life extension.','A permanently sealed join could simplify assembly but make repair and end-of-life separation harder.','Prototype the clip for repeated use and document replacement cost.','A reusable label alone does not establish sustainability over a full life cycle.'),
('Three concepts use folded sheet material, modular panels or a single moulded body to solve the same storage need.','Different structural approaches expand the solution space before one option is selected.','Divergent ideation generates meaningfully different mechanisms rather than cosmetic variations.','Changing only the colour of one concept would not explore the same breadth of structural possibilities.','Combine a promising folding mechanism with replaceable modules and sketch its operation.','More ideas do not guarantee better ideas; evaluation must return to requirements.'),
('A sketch shows a folding hinge in two positions, with arrows for movement and labelled dimensions.','The sketch communicates mechanism and scale rather than appearance alone.','Multiple states, movement arrows and dimensions support interpretation of function.','One decorative perspective without hinge detail would be less useful for testing operation.','Add a section through the hinge and identify the material and clearance needed.','A sketch can propose operation but cannot prove durability without a prototype.'),
('Concept A costs $18 and fails a leak test; B costs $23 and passes. The brief requires no leaks and a maximum $25 price.','B currently meets both supplied mandatory criteria; A meets price but fails the leak requirement.','Criterion-based comparison prevents a low price from compensating for a mandatory failure.','Choosing A only because it is cheaper would disregard the no-leak requirement.','Retest B with repeated use and different orientations before final selection.','One successful test does not establish long-term reliability or all life-cycle effects.'),
('A prototype’s lid leaks after 50 cycles. A revised seal passes 100 cycles but is difficult to replace.','Refinement solves one failure while creating a repairability trade-off.','Iteration must evaluate interacting requirements rather than one test result alone.','A permanent adhesive seal might stop leaks but further reduce repairability.','Develop a replaceable seal and test leakage, removal effort and cost together.','The final recommendation should identify remaining uncertainty and the evidence needed.')]
}
for sid,rows in arts.items():
 for i,args in enumerate(rows):interpret_set(sid,i,*args)
